# tools/l/lint_surface_census_witness.rish -- which rows of the lint table does a lap actually run?
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/l/lint_surface_census_witness.rish
#
# WHAT THIS GUARDS. `context/TAME_GUIDANCE.md` carries the tree's lint surface as a table under the
# heading **Enforced now**, and beneath it a paragraph naming what actually holds those rows -- read
# `20260911.112513`, and written down rather than assumed, which was the right instinct. It is a
# hand-typed census carried inside prose, and the mark law already rules on that shape: COUNT, NEVER
# NUMBER. A total inside a sentence stays at whatever it was on the day somebody typed it.
#
# IT WENT STALE IN ONE DAY. Measured over the guide's own history: 11 tools named and 5 rostered at
# `20260911.050903`, 13 and 6 at `140923`, **14 and 7** today. Two rows seated later the same day
# each arrived with a rostered guard beside it -- the tree working exactly as it should, and nothing
# recomputing the sentence that described it.
#
# AND THE HEADLINE NEVER SIGNALLED THE DRIFT. Rows read TWENTY at every one of those commits and
# reads twenty today, so the first figure a reader spot-checks agrees -- which buys the breakdown
# beneath it a trust it has stopped earning. A census whose total stays right while its parts move
# is worse than one that goes visibly wrong, because nothing in it looks wrong.
#
# WHAT THE INSTRUMENT ADDS OVER THE SENTENCE. Three states rather than two. A row is `rostered` when
# it names a tool the standing roster carries by `path`; `driven` when it names an unrostered tool
# BESIDE a rostered one in the same row, which is the table's own reading of the bans scan; and
# `unreached` when no lap runs anything it names. That middle state is why the paragraph's SIX is
# right where a naive count of unrostered tools reads seven, and a guard blind to it would call an
# honest row broken and be turned off in a week.
#
# THE DERIVED SIX ARE THE HAND-NAMED SIX, exactly -- cellar_first_ring, claim_preserve,
# dated_guard, designed_not_built, proven_seat, radiant_lint. Agreement on the population is what
# makes the disagreement on the counts worth trusting.
#
# WHY ONE WALL AND ONE RATCHET. A table citing a tool absent from disk tells a reader to run
# something that cannot run, repairable on the lap it lands, so **zero**. A row no lap runs is often
# correct and reasoned -- claim_preserve refuses a bare invocation by design, designed_not_built
# waits on a ruling, proven_seat wants a staged bench -- so a wall there would red on rows nobody
# may repair, and a wall that reds on honest work is a wall somebody turns off.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT REACH. Whether a rostered guard actually CHECKS the rule its row claims. A tool
# may run every lap and read a different question than the row beside it names. This asks only
# whether a lap runs anything the row points at.
#
# Purely local -- no key, no network, no funds, no device. It reads two tracked files and a pen.

say "lint-surface: Language EN -- a census typed into prose stops counting the day it is written."
say "lint-surface: Style Gauge, Meter -- both fault populations are named beside their totals, since a bare count is visible the way a locked door is."
say "lint-surface: Lens TAME -- docs and implementation stay synced; assert it rather than assume it."

# 1 -- the pen. Every refusal planted and then lifted, and every state proven from both sides, so a
# reader that always says `held` is caught by the same bytes that prove `held` works.
let control = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/l/lint_surface_census_control.sh"]
say control.out
say control.err
assert control.ok else "lint-surface: a control case read the wrong way: ${control.out} ${control.err}"
assert control.out contains "control_failed=0" else "lint-surface: the pen must balance before the field is believed: ${control.out}"
assert control.out contains "legs=46" else "lint-surface: a leg left the pen unheard -- `control_failed=0` reads alike over a leg that failed and one deleted: ${control.out}"

# 2 -- the field.
let field = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/l/lint_surface_census_scan.sh"]
say field.out
say field.err
assert field.ok else "lint-surface: the field reading refused: ${field.out} ${field.err}"
assert field.out contains "tools_missing=0" else "lint-surface: the lint table cites a tool that is not on disk, so a reader is told to run something that cannot run: ${field.out}"
assert field.out contains "unheld_ceiling=9" else "lint-surface: the ceiling moved -- it may fall when a row earns a rostered guard, and never rise: ${field.out}"
assert field.out contains "verdict=ok" else "lint-surface: a row of the lint surface has lost its instrument, or one it cites has left the tree: ${field.out}"

# 3 -- the reach, asserted rather than assumed. A walk that found nothing reports a perfect zero
# (REDS %463), so the population is named here rather than trusted.
assert (field.out contains "rows=0") == false else "lint-surface: the table walk read no rule row at all, so its reading means nothing: ${field.out}"
assert (field.out contains "tools_rostered=0") == false else "lint-surface: not one cited tool matched the roster, which is a broken reader wearing a finding's clothes: ${field.out}"
assert field.out contains "unreached_list:" else "lint-surface: the unreached population is named every run rather than behind a verb, since its size alone locates nothing: ${field.out}"
assert field.out contains "missing_list:" else "lint-surface: the missing population is named every run for the same reason: ${field.out}"

say "GREEN: lint-surface -- every row of the lint table names a tool that exists, and the ones no lap runs are named rather than counted."
